+
+NOTES = r"""
+Definition. Section 1 defines r-Set Packing with alpha()-Overlap: choose at
+least k input sets such that every pair has alpha=0. Definition 1 in Section 2
+defines a well-conditioned overlap function. Section 4.1, Lemma 7 proves that
+alpha-Weight is well-conditioned for nonnegative weights. This module gives
+every universe element weight one and uses threshold zero, so alpha=0 means
+the selected sets are disjoint. These are the paper's native set-system
+objects; no graph, SAT, or finite-field surrogate is handed to the solver.
+
+Step-0 algorithm audit. Section 1 says the generalized problems inherit
+NP-completeness from r-Set Packing. Theorem 3.2 gives the relevant easy route:
+for fixed r, the BST-alpha algorithm runs in
+O(r^(rk) k^((r+1)k) n^(cr)), hence it is fixed-parameter tractable in k.
+Accordingly k grows from 3 to 24 with n on the named ladder; claiming hardness
+from a large universe while keeping k tiny would contradict the point of that
+result. Section 3.2's graph-to-set reduction is not used.
+
+Generation. First sample k set labels uniformly. They are forced to be an
+independent class of an r-regular incidence graph. All planted vertices send
+r edges to decoy vertices. Decoy cross-degrees are balanced to differ by at
+most one; their remaining degrees are joined internally. Degree-preserving
+edge switches randomize both parts. Finally each graph edge is renamed as one
+universe element and becomes incident with precisely its two endpoint sets.
+The planted labels therefore give pairwise-disjoint sets by construction.
+Every input set has size exactly r and every element has frequency exactly two,
+so planted sets have no size, frequency, input-position, or degree outlier.
