+Generation is inverse. It first samples a connected regular constraint graph and
+a cyclic sheet offset for every candidate group. Observable option `a` in group
+`i` represents sheet `(a + offset[i]) mod q`. For every forbidden option pair on
+a constraint edge, the builder creates one positive-weight element shared by
+exactly those two sets. Each group also has a common positive anchor, and all sets
+share one zero-weight element. Any one global sheet is a certified packing; the
+stored answer is sheet zero and is known before any sets are assembled.
+
+The anchor’s weight residue modulo `q` equals its group offset. Random positive
+weight quotients and arbitrary element relabelling preserve equal sizes and remove
+simple magnitude/position outliers. The first two displayed groups are a nonedge
+with distinct, individually uniform offsets, which prevents the no-backtracking
+left-to-right greedy prefix from completing.
 
 ## Why this is Track B
 
-Definition 1 in Section 2 fixes “well-conditioned”: heredity, conflicts only on
-nonempty overlaps with a removable conflicting element, and polynomial-time
-evaluation. Lemma 7 in Section 4.1 proves that the nonnegative `alpha`-Weight
-predicate used here has those properties.
+Definition 1 in Section 2 says a well-conditioned overlap rule is hereditary,
+conflicts only on a nonempty overlap with a removable conflicting element, and
+is polynomial-time computable. Lemma 7 in Section 4.1 proves that the nonnegative
+`alpha`-Weight predicate used here has those properties.
 
-The paper itself also identifies the easy algorithmic regime. Theorems 3.1 and
-3.2 in Section 3.1 give the BST-`alpha` algorithm and the bound
-`O(r^(rk) k^((r+1)k) N^(cr))`; in particular, small packing size `k` is FPT.
-Consequently this family makes no Track A claim. Here the paper's `k` is the
-generator's growing `n`.
+The paper’s easy-result warning is central: Theorems 3.1 and 3.2 in Section 3.1
+give the BST-`alpha` algorithm with running time
+`O(r^(rk) k^((r+1)k) N^(cr))`; small packing size `k` is FPT. This family therefore
+makes no Track A claim, and the paper’s `k` grows with the generator’s `n`.
 
-The measured exact reference algorithm builds every local compatibility table
-and propagates a component in `O(|E| q^2 r)`. At the provisional hard preset it
-solves 8/8 instances, averaging 7,168 candidate-pair comparisons, 204,528
-positive-element membership probes, 32 component nodes, and 0.0923 seconds in
-CPython. A solver that recognizes the covering decomposition need only follow
-one sheet: at most `(n-1)(q+1) = 279` exact comparisons/assignments. The gap is
-the Track B claim: the mechanical solver is easy for a computer but cannot be
-executed unaided in context, while the compact route fits the no-tool cap.
+The measured reference algorithm constructs every local compatibility table and
+propagates a connected component in `O(|E| q^2 r)`. On eight hard instances it
+solved 8/8, averaging 7,168 candidate-pair comparisons, 204,635.875 exact
+positive-element probes, 32 component nodes, and 0.0469 seconds in CPython. The
+compact route notices the anchor invariant and chooses option
+`(-anchor_weight) mod q` in each group: 64 modular operations at shipping size, also
